  "query_preview": "..Seeing widespread valley rain and mountain snow\nacross Utah and southwest Wyoming this morning with ample moisture\nand instability in place from the combination of a landfalling\natmospheric river and a strong trough over the Great Basin. The\ncold front associated with the storm system is currently in the\nvicinity of the Utah/Nevada border with this boundary moving\nacross the area during the day as the system continues eastward.\nThus, widespread coverage of precipitation will continue\nthrough the day and into tonight, with snow levels steadily\nlowering into the 6,500 to 7,000 foot range. All Utah mountains\nwill see accumulating snow, with significant accumulations for\nareas favored in northwest flow, particularly for the Wasatch\nMountains south of I-80 as well as the western Uintas. Going\nWinter Weather Advisories cover the current threat well.\n\nIn southern Utah, flooding is ongoing in a variety of areas\nincluding Zion National Park, the Paria River, and the Escalante\nRiver. With rain continuing to fall steadily throughout the day,\nadditional areas of flooding area likely through the afternoon and\na Flood Watch for southern Utah remains in effect through the\nevening. Already high flows and saturated soils from recent \nprevious flooding events will only exacerbate the problem.\n\nMonday morning, the Pacific trough is expected to quickly exit the\narea as high pressure moves in behind it. The vast majority of\nprecipitation is expected to end by midday, with perhaps a few\nshowers hanging around over the higher terrain of northern Utah\nMonday afternoon. The ridge will remain in place through Tuesday,\nresulting in dry and unseasonably cool temperatures with maxes\nrunning up to 15F below seasonal normals. \n\nGuidance indicates that the ridge will break down and shift east\nby Wednesday as another Pacific trough moves across the area.\nConfidence is increasing that associated moisture with this system\nwill be significantly less than the last few systems, leading to a\nmuch more limited threat of precipitation. The primary impact is\nexpected to be on temperatures, which should remain quite\nunseasonably cool into the latter part of the week. Behind that\nsystem, drier conditions are expected to prevail with the\npotential of a blocking ridge developing off the Pacific coast. In\na northerly flow, temperatures are still expected to remain on the\ncool side into the upcoming weekend.",
